  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Why Is My Kitchen Trim Not Matching?",
    "text": "Why Is My Kitchen Trim Not Matching?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "It can be frustrating when your kitchen trim doesn't quite match the rest of your space, sticking out like a sore thumb instead of blending in. There are a few common reasons why this might happen with finish carpentry. Often, it comes down to a mismatch in material or profile. If new trim is installed, and it has a slightly different shape or wood species than existing elements, it will be noticeable. Another big culprit is color or sheen discrepancy. Even if the profiles are similar, a fresh coat of paint or stain that isn't perfectly matched to older, faded finishes can create a stark contrast. The way light hits different finishes can also play a role, making a seemingly identical color appear different. Installation quality matters, too. Visible gaps at joints, poor scribing where the trim meets uneven walls, or overfilled nail holes can all detract from a cohesive look. Precision is everything in finish carpentry, from the initial measurements to the final caulking and touch-ups. If you’re struggling to get your kitchen trim to look unified, especially with custom work or trying to blend new with old, a seasoned finish carpenter can pinpoint the issues and achieve that seamless integration you’re aiming for.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}